Background and setup. A causal set is a locally finite partially ordered set $(C,\preceq)$, intended as a discrete model of Lorentzian spacetime in which $x\preceq y$ encodes causal precedence. For a scalar field $\phi:C\to\mathbb{R}$ on a causal set sprinkled (via a Poisson process of unit density) into $D$-dimensional Minkowski spacetime, one can define a family of retarded, Lorentz-invariant, nonlocal linear operators
$$
(B^{(D)}\phi)(x) \;=\; \frac{1}{\ell^{2}}\!\left(\,a\,\phi(x) \;+\; \sum_{k}\,b_{k}\sum_{y\in L_{k}(x)}\phi(y)\right),
$$
where $\ell$ is a nonlocality length scale, $L_{k}(x)$ denotes the $k$-th causal "layer" of points to the past of $x$ (defined via the order interval cardinalities $|I(y,x)|=k-1$), and the constants $a,b_{k}$ are chosen so that, after averaging over sprinklings, the operator converges in the continuum limit to the standard d'Alembertian $\Box$ acting on smooth scalar fields. These nonlocal operators $B^{(D)}$ provide a causal-set analogue of $\Box$ and underlie a class of "off-shell" propagation models proposed as candidates for dark matter phenomenology.

Problem. Construct (or prove the existence/non-existence of) a generalization of the nonlocal causal-set d'Alembertian framework above to non-scalar fields. Specifically, formulate retarded, Lorentz-invariant, nonlocal linear differential-type operators on causal sets that act on:

(a) vector fields (sections of a bundle over the causal set that limits, in the continuum, to the tangent or cotangent bundle of Minkowski spacetime), reproducing in the continuum the appropriate wave operator on $1$-forms or vectors;

(b) spinor fields, reproducing in the continuum the Dirac operator (or its square) on spinor fields in $D$-dimensional Minkowski spacetime;

(c) tensor fields of arbitrary rank.

In each case, the construction must:
(i) be defined intrinsically on the causal set, using only the order relation and discrete combinatorial data of the sprinkling;
(ii) be retarded (depending only on the causal past of each point);
(iii) be Lorentz invariant in expectation under the Poisson sprinkling;
(iv) converge, in mean, to the corresponding continuum wave operator as the discreteness scale tends to zero with $\ell$ held fixed (or in an appropriate continuum limit).

Determine whether such a construction is possible, and if so, exhibit it explicitly.

